Relay the OAuth callback into the app, and take Microsoft's name off the page.

callback.html hands the result back by writing to localStorage, which the
workbench then reads. That never happens here: the callback lands in the system
browser while the workbench runs in a WebView, and the two are separate storage
origins, so every sign-in flow hangs waiting for a value that was written
somewhere else.

intent:// rather than a custom scheme, because Chrome refuses to navigate to one
from script without a user gesture while intent:// is always permitted and
resolves through the manifest intent-filter. Synchronously rather than from a
timeout, because the activation that permits the navigation is gone by the time
one would fire. The localStorage write is left in place so the flow still works
if the page is ever opened in the WebView itself.

The naming matters more here than anywhere else in the tree, which is why it is
in this patch rather than in 0011: this is the one page of the product that is
rendered by the user's own browser rather than inside the app, at the end of every
extension sign-in, with a title in the browser's tab bar.

The third piece of Microsoft branding on this page is not here. Its `.branding`
rule carries the VS Code logo as a 150 KB base64 PNG on one line, and a diff that
removes a line has to quote it. The Branding stage of scripts/build-vscode-oss.sh
strips it instead, and verify-server-tree.py asserts on both sides of the pivot
that the packaged page carries no embedded image and no Microsoft product name.

